{
  "placeholder_items": true,
  "simulated_students": true,
  "no_llm": true,
  "assumptions": [
    "Simulated difficulty is not real difficulty: the chance of a right answer per level is chosen in configs/ladder_eval.json, not measured from students. The medium row equals the old answer model (slip 0.10, guess 0.15). Nothing here shows that AI-labelled hard questions are harder.",
    "Self-report is chosen too: yes_if_known and yes_if_not_known in the config, and a student who does not say yes picks No or Not sure at random. Both start at easy.",
    "A student's knowledge of a topic is two states, known or not known, from true mastery above 0.8. Real knowledge has degrees, so Quick revision and Study this cannot be scored as such: both count as not known.",
    "Answers are independent given the topic and level, so a right answer on a topic does not make the next one likelier. A real student's answers are linked, so the benefit of asking a second or third question is probably an upper limit.",
    "Placeholder quiz items: one easy, medium and hard item per topic, with no real text. Real courses will have missing levels; no_hard_items shows the worst case.",
    "The example graph: 5 topics on the target's path. Rerun on real course graphs before citing.",
    "Mastery values per result (0.9, 0.7, 0.4, 0.1) only place Know it above the 0.8 threshold. They are a design choice, not calibrated probabilities.",
    "adaptive_levelled uses the same answer model and items as the ladders, and asks items in file order, so easy first. adaptive is the existing strategy on the existing quiz and answer model.",
    "A question is one quiz question. Self-report taps are counted apart."
  ],
